\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Convention}
\title{Fixed-budget trace-selection protocol}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}
\tag{harness}

\p{The trace intervention is a finite evaluation proposal. It compares
horizon and selector effects at a fixed retained budget while keeping the
model, teacher, scaffold, task split, verifier, seed law, and total token and
compute budgets visible. The source's selective-refinement intervention
supplies the comparison pattern, but the following arm matrix is an FTIP
protocol rather than a source result
\citet{Sections 6-7; Tables 4-5, 10}{dang2026selecting}.}

\p{Cross horizon and selection with the state masks in
\ref{ftip-00P0}, which retain or clear session context, candidate history,
workspace, and the verified best candidate. At minimum compare:}
\ol{
  \li{one-stage full-long traces;}
  \li{verification-preserving intermediate truncation;}
  \li{warm-start plus top-likelihood selection;}
  \li{warm-start plus bottom-likelihood selection;}
  \li{warm-start plus random selection at the same retained budget; and}
  \li{a coverage-stratified or diversity-preserving selector.}
}

\p{For every arm, report successful tasks and attempts, repeatability,
premature-stop, productive-persistence, over-persistence, recovery,
verification success, task and strategy coverage, trace tokens, total training
compute, wall time, storage, and selection overhead. Pair every selected trace
with its unselected continuation and reserve held-out tasks and a
cross-benchmark split. Score only information available at selection time and
prevent verifier outcomes from leaking into the selection label.}

\p{The protocol must charge selection, storage, restart, and discarded-trace
costs and retain the task, selector, budget, split, and seed receipt. A claim
that selected traces improve FTIP behavior waits for an immutable experiment
record with those fields; the present card states a future-research gate.}
